                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_diagnosed:     `<strong>That’s the architect’s first job.</strong> You separated the stated brief from the diagnosed challenge: this is a behaviour problem — managers not making cross-functional calls or holding accountability — not a finance-and-strategy knowledge gap. Now every later decision can be designed against a real target instead of an order ticket.`,
                    coach_r1_stated:        `<strong>You’ve built exactly what HR asked for.</strong> Finance, strategy and a communication module — the participants will love it. But I said the problem is managers not making cross-functional decisions or holding accountability. Your programme teaches none of that directly. At the 90-day checkpoint, what behaviour will have changed?`,
                    coach_r1_topics:        `<strong>More modules isn’t more value.</strong> Packing in extra leadership topics looks generous and addresses none of the diagnosed gap. Volume of content is not the same as alignment to the business challenge — and it’s the alignment I’ll judge you on.`,
                    coach_r1_knowledge:     `<strong>A knowledge outcome won’t survive the board.</strong> “Managers will understand frameworks” is Kirkpatrick Level 2 — I asked for leaders who can <em>run a P&L</em>. Write the outcome as the on-the-job behaviour I can see at 90 days, or you’re measuring the wrong thing from the start.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Real need named, target written as a measurable on-the-job behaviour, comfort content trimmed. That’s the linkage from business challenge to learning outcome that custom exec-ed lives or dies on — and it lowers renewal risk from the first decision.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>Blended is the architecture that fits.</strong> At ~£1,900/head it serves all 220 within the envelope, your top faculty anchor the in-person intensives, and the workplace projects are where behaviour change actually lives. Cost, reach and impact balanced — exactly the trade-off the format choice is supposed to make.`,
                    coach_r2_residential:   `<strong>A fully residential design needs £748,000 for 220.</strong> You have £480,000. You can serve 141 of them, or you can find a format that scales. Which 79 managers do I tell my board you won’t develop? Premium is an aspiration; the budget is the constraint.`,
                    coach_r2_digital:       `<strong>Cheapest and most scalable — and weakest on behaviour.</strong> Self-paced digital with coaching keeps you well under budget, but you’ve traded away the in-person intensives and the workplace pull that move behaviour. You’ll have margin and a happy finance team, and no behaviour shift at 90 days.`,
                    coach_r2_overbook:      `<strong>Selling a 40-day design on 18 available days is over-promising.</strong> When the calendar bites you’ll backfill with whoever’s free, the quality the bid was won on evaporates, and the evidence at 90 days suffers. Reconcile demand to the faculty you actually have.`,
                    coach_r2_dilute:        `<strong>Stripping out your best faculty hits the day count and the impact.</strong> Cheaper associates across the board protect the budget but dilute the very expertise that converts a P&L case into a manager’s decision. Leverage your top three on the intensives instead of replacing them.`,
                    coach_r2_designheavy:   `<strong>£220,000 on bespoke design is the content reflex.</strong> Design is what clients praise in the room, but at this share you’re crowding out the measurement and application that earn renewal. Lean design, funded evidence — that’s the allocation that wins the next contract.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Your allocation exceeds the fixed £480,000.</strong> The board set that number and it does not move. A proposal that doesn’t fit the envelope isn’t a proposal — it’s a negotiation you’ve already lost. Bring the total back inside the line.`,
                    coach_r3_impact:        `<strong>That’s impact engineered, not appended.</strong> Level 3–4 measurement funded, real workplace application built in, and metrics anchored to the behaviour outcome. You’ll be able to stand in front of my board at 90 days and <em>show</em> what changed — which is exactly how this account renews.`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>You stripped the £45,000 plan to add two more modules.</strong> Smart on paper. But renewal depends on <em>evidence</em> of behaviour change at 90 days. You’ve built a programme you can’t prove worked — when my board asks “did it change anything?”, what do you show them?`,
                    coach_r3_level1:        `<strong>Happy-sheets and a quiz aren’t evidence.</strong> Levels 1–2 tell me people enjoyed it and remembered it — not that they’re running a P&L differently. Without Level 3–4 you’re measuring satisfaction and calling it impact, and the renewal gate is behaviour.`,
                    coach_r3_noapp:         `<strong>Excellent modules and zero application back at work — that’s the 10 of 70-20-10.</strong> Knowledge will go up; behaviour won’t move, because nothing makes managers practise it on the job. Where in your design does the learning meet the manager’s actual P&L decision?`,
                    coach_r3_optional:      `<strong>An optional project nobody owns won’t shift behaviour.</strong> Without required line-manager involvement and spaced practice, the application is the first thing a busy manager drops. The 70-20-10 mechanism only works when it’s built in, not suggested.`,
                    coach_r3_reaction:      `<strong>Course-satisfaction scores look good in a board pack and prove nothing.</strong> They’re Level 1. Anchor your metrics to the 90-day cross-functional-decision and accountability evidence — the behaviour I said the renewal depends on.`,
                    coach_r3_lowsplit:      `<strong>Almost nothing committed to measurement and application.</strong> That’s the content reflex again — every pound on more modules is a pound not spent on the evidence and the workplace pull that earn renewal. In custom exec-ed, measurement isn’t overhead.`,
                    coach_r3_overbudget:    `<strong>Adding measurement and application pushed you over the £480,000.</strong> The right components, the wrong allocation. Something funded earlier — likely heavy bespoke design — has to give, because the envelope does not.`,
                    coach_r4_behaviour:     `<strong>That’s the argument the board buys.</strong> Diagnosed need, a format that converts learning to on-the-job action, and evidence at 90 days — one coherent case, managed to the KPI that actually gates renewal. Lead with this and the £1.4M conversation is about <em>when</em>, not <em>whether</em>.`,
                    coach_r4_satisfaction:  `<strong>You’re optimising for great reviews.</strong> Satisfaction is Kirkpatrick Level 1; my renewal is gated on Level 3 behaviour. A delighted cohort that doesn’t change behaviour still loses you the account. Which KPI are you actually managing to?`,
                    coach_r4_content:       `<strong>Walking my board through every module is the designer’s pitch, not the sponsor’s.</strong> I don’t renew on curriculum breadth — I renew on whether these managers run a P&L differently in 90 days. Lead with the behaviour case, not the module list.`,
                    coach_r4_overpromise:   `<strong>You’ve guaranteed a large measurable lift for £2,182 a head. Bold.</strong> If you miss, you lose the £1.4M three-year account and your evidence becomes a case study in failure. Are these the outcomes the programme can deliver, or the ones that win the bid?`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“Positive feedback and engagement” commits to nothing — and earns nothing.</strong> A promise I can’t hold you to is a promise I can’t renew on. Commit to a credible, evidenced behaviour shift the design can actually produce.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That’s how an architect defends a budget.</strong> You named what you trimmed — the comfort content — and why measurement and application were protected. Owning the trade-off is what tells my board the £480,000 was allocated to win renewal, not to win the bid.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>“We funded everything” on a fixed £480,000 isn’t credible.</strong> Every pound on content is a pound not on measurement; I know there were trade-offs. Pretending there weren’t tells me you either don’t understand the constraint or won’t be honest about it.`,
                    coach_r4_defendcontent: `<strong>Defending the cut measurement is defending the wrong call.</strong> More content was never the smarter buy when renewal is gated on evidence. You’re asking me to fund a programme you can’t prove worked — and I won’t.`,

