                    insight_won:            `Account won: you ran the genuine architect’s pattern — diagnosed the behaviour gap behind the brief, chose a blended format that served all 220 within the fixed £480,000, funded Level 3–4 measurement, and built 70-20-10 workplace application so behaviour actually shifts. The 90-day evidence will hold, and the £1.4M three-year account renews on proof, not on reviews.`,
                    insight_ordertaking:    `Order-taking: you designed to Halford’s stated brief — finance, strategy, communication — instead of the diagnosed behaviour challenge. A well-reviewed programme that teaches none of what the sponsor said the business needed. At 90 days there is no cross-functional-decision behaviour to show, and the renewal goes to a competitor.`,
                    insight_budget:         `Budget blown: the design didn’t fit the board-set £480,000. A fully residential or over-allocated proposal at 220 heads either overspends or quietly drops managers — and a proposal that doesn’t respect a fixed envelope loses before the behaviour case is even heard.`,
                    insight_measurement:    `Cannot prove it: you cut or under-built the Level 3–4 evidence plan to fund content. The programme may even have worked — but with no behaviour measurement at 90 days you can’t show it, and in custom exec-ed an unprovable result is an unrenewed account.`,
                    insight_application:    `Knowledge, not behaviour: the design is content-rich with no 70-20-10 workplace mechanism. Knowledge rose; behaviour didn’t, because nothing made managers practise cross-functional decisions on the job. The 90-day evidence shows learning without change.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed proposal: parts of the architecture landed, but the diagnosis, format, measurement and application didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough gaps that the sponsor can’t be confident the 90-day evidence will hold, and renewal stays in doubt.`,
                    insight_satisfaction:   `Liked but unchanged: you optimised for great reviews (Level 1) while the renewal gate was behaviour (Level 3). A delighted cohort that runs its P&L exactly as before still loses the account — satisfaction is the KPI that feels like success and isn’t.`,
                    insight_overpromise:    `Over-promised: you guaranteed outcomes to win the bid that the design can’t credibly deliver. If the 90-day evidence falls short of the guarantee, the miss is on the record and the relationship — and the £1.4M — is harder to recover than if you’d set a credible target.`,
                    insight_behaviour_low:  `Projected behaviour change stayed low: the architecture never built a credible path from the classroom to the manager’s on-the-job decision, so the renewal-gating KPI is the weakest number in the pack.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You exceeded the fixed envelope: committed spend ran past £480,000, which the board set and will not move. Even a strong design is rejected when it doesn’t fit the constraint the whole brief was built around.`,
